From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: adbtrr_work6_pr_fixes_use <>
Date: Thu, 6 Aug 2026 12:52:47 +0000
Subject: [PATCH] os/mm: Repair the poison pattern after reporting a
 use-after-free

mm_uaf_verify() reported the corruption and returned without touching
the chunk. The chunk is not always consumed by the caller: mm_free()
and mm_realloc() verify a neighbour which usually stays on the free
list. The corrupted bytes therefore stayed corrupted, and every later
free or realloc touching that neighbour re-detected and re-printed the
same report, forever, from a single defect.

Re-fill the window with the pattern once the report has been printed.
A second report now means a genuinely new write instead of an echo of
the first one, which makes the report count meaningful. The repair is
skipped when CONFIG_DEBUG_MM_UAF_PANIC is set, since the crash dump
should show the corrupting value.

Also add CONFIG_DEBUG_MM_UAF_MAX_REPORTS as a console budget for a
defect which fires continuously. Detection and repair keep running when
the budget is spent, only the printing stops. The default of 0 keeps
every report, so behaviour is unchanged unless it is configured.
---
 os/Kconfig.debug       | 10 ++++++++++
 os/mm/mm_heap/mm_uaf.c | 38 ++++++++++++++++++++++++++++++++++++++
 2 files changed, 48 insertions(+)

diff --git a/os/Kconfig.debug b/os/Kconfig.debug
index 6e934462..b69e2549 100644
--- a/os/Kconfig.debug
+++ b/os/Kconfig.debug
@@ -830,6 +830,16 @@ config DEBUG_MM_UAF_DUMP_SIZE
 		console to trip the watchdog. The poisoned window is always dumped even
 		if this value is smaller than the window itself.
 
+config DEBUG_MM_UAF_MAX_REPORTS
+	int "Maximum number of reports to print (0 for no limit)"
+	default 0
+	range 0 65535
+	---help---
+		Stop printing after this many use-after-free reports. The detection
+		and the repair of the poison pattern always keep running, only the
+		printing stops, so a defect which fires continuously cannot take over
+		the console. Zero, the default, prints every report.
+
 config DEBUG_MM_UAF_PANIC
 	bool "Panic when use-after-free is detected"
 	default n
diff --git a/os/mm/mm_heap/mm_uaf.c b/os/mm/mm_heap/mm_uaf.c
index 8eb7f94b..31e7bed0 100644
--- a/os/mm/mm_heap/mm_uaf.c
+++ b/os/mm/mm_heap/mm_uaf.c
@@ -30,6 +30,18 @@
 
 #include "mm_node.h"
 
+/****************************************************************************
+ * Private Data
+ ****************************************************************************/
+
+#if CONFIG_DEBUG_MM_UAF_MAX_REPORTS > 0
+/* Number of reports printed so far. Only used to stop a repeating writer from
+ * owning the console; the detection itself is never suppressed.
+ */
+
+static uint32_t g_uaf_nreports;
+#endif
+
 /****************************************************************************
  * Private Functions
  ****************************************************************************/
@@ -145,6 +157,20 @@ void mm_uaf_verify(struct mm_freenode_s *node)
 		return;
 	}
 
+#if CONFIG_DEBUG_MM_UAF_MAX_REPORTS > 0
+	if (g_uaf_nreports >= CONFIG_DEBUG_MM_UAF_MAX_REPORTS) {
+		/* The console budget is spent. Still repair the pattern below so that
+		 * the allocator does not keep tripping over the same bytes, but do not
+		 * print anything more.
+		 */
+
+		mm_uaf_poison(node);
+		return;
+	}
+
+	g_uaf_nreports++;
+#endif
+
 	/* Decide how much of the chunk to dump. A small chunk is shown in full so
 	 * that the bytes around the corruption stay visible, but the dump is never
 	 * longer than CONFIG_DEBUG_MM_UAF_DUMP_SIZE. Without that cap a corrupted
@@ -169,5 +195,17 @@ void mm_uaf_verify(struct mm_freenode_s *node)
 
 #ifdef CONFIG_DEBUG_MM_UAF_PANIC
 	PANIC();
+#else
+	/* Repair the pattern now that the corruption has been reported.
+	 *
+	 * The chunk is not necessarily consumed by the caller: mm_free() and
+	 * mm_realloc() verify a neighbour which often stays on the free list.
+	 * Without the repair, every later free or realloc touching that neighbour
+	 * re-detects the same bytes and prints the same report forever. With the
+	 * repair, a second report means a genuinely new write rather than an echo
+	 * of the first one, so the report count becomes meaningful.
+	 */
+
+	mm_uaf_poison(node);
 #endif
 }
